 x + 5$, then $f = O(1)$ as $x → 0$.\n\nA proof of the statement is given below.\n\n> $L1:4x^{2} = O(1)$ as $x → 0$\n> $L2: - x = O(1)$ as $x → 0$\n> $L3:5 = O(1)$ as $x → 0$\n> $L4:f = O(3)$ as $x → 0$\n> $L5:f = O(1)$ as $x → 0$\n\nSelect the correct options to justify each step of the reasoning.\n\nLine $L4$ follows from lines\n{{field-1}}\nand\n{{field-2}}.\n\nLine $L5$ follows from line\n{{field-3}}\nand\n{{field-4}}."}]}
